From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 02:39:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Windows: check code bytes in an untrappable WoW64 host page
 on iOS

The invalidation tracker traps a write to translated RWX code by making
its pages read-only (0033: every RWX page of the host page). On iOS a
host page is 16 KiB, and in the WoW64 window Wine gives it the union of
its four guest pages' protections. When one of the other guest pages
is committed writable but not RWX (a data page of the same allocation
or image), the host page stays writable whatever FEX does: a store to
the code does not fault, and without FlushInstructionCache the old
translation runs. The childabort probe's noflush phase (code in page 0
of a 16 KiB allocation whose pages 1-3 are read-write data) returned
the old value on 98% of its i386 calls.

Such a host page cannot be trapped, so its code is checked instead:
for the WOW64 module, QueryExecutableRange splits an RWX range at a
host page it covers in part that holds a committed writable page
outside the RWX ranges, and reports that part as FullSMC; the frontend
then compiles its blocks with ForceFullSMCDetection (each instruction
compares its bytes, as for Mono's tailcall sites), and the disk cache
is not used for it. A range aligned to host pages costs nothing; a
partial one costs up to three NtQueryVirtualMemory calls per range
query. A page made writable beside RWX code invalidates the host
page's code, so it is compiled again with the checks. With SMC
detection off (Mono) nothing changes.

The x86-64 (ARM64EC) process is not changed: there the trap does make
the host page read-only, and Wine's write-strip heal of the writable
neighbour makes it writable again; the same checks there deadlocked
the first thread whose check failed. Off iOS nothing changes.

Class: upstream-bug
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-13-smc-host-page.md
Offered-upstream: no; FEX's Windows frontend assumes 4 KiB host pages
